  <h2>Average and Median Salary in Canada</h2>
  <p>
    According to Statistics Canada, the average annual salary for full-time employees in Canada is approximately
    $64,850 as of 2025. However, the average can be misleading because high earners pull the figure up. The
    median salary -- the point at which half of workers earn more and half earn less -- is approximately
    $40,500 per year. This gap between the average and median highlights the income inequality in the Canadian
    labour market.
  </p>
  <p>
    Understanding where your salary falls relative to these benchmarks helps with career planning, salary
    negotiations, and financial decisions. These figures are based on the Labour Force Survey and the Canadian
    Income Survey published by Statistics Canada.
  </p>

  <h2>Average Salary by Province</h2>
  <p>
    Salaries vary significantly across provinces, driven by differences in industry composition, cost of living,
    and labour demand. Resource-rich provinces and territories tend to have higher average earnings, while
    Atlantic provinces generally have lower averages.
  </p>
