      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Men</td><td>$70,200</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Women</td><td>$58,800</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>How Average Salary Translates to Take-Home Pay</h2>
  <p>
    The national average salary of $64,850 does not represent what a typical worker actually takes home.
    After federal and provincial income taxes, CPP contributions, and EI premiums, the take-home pay on
    $64,850 varies by province. For example:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ontario:</strong> Approximately $51,200 net per year ($4,267 per month)</li>
    <li><strong>Alberta:</strong> Approximately $52,800 net per year ($4,400 per month)</li>
    <li><strong>Quebec:</strong> Approximately $48,400 net per year ($4,033 per month)</li>
    <li><strong>British Columbia:</strong> Approximately $51,600 net per year ($4,300 per month)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Use our <a href="/">salary calculator</a> to calculate your exact take-home pay based on your province,
    salary, and personal circumstances.
  </p>

  <h2>What Is Considered a Good Salary in Canada?</h2>
  <p>
    A good salary depends on where you live, your household size, and your financial goals. As a general
    guide, earning above the median salary ($40,500) means you earn more than half of Canadian workers.
    Earning above the average ($64,850) puts you in the upper portion of the income distribution. In
    expensive cities like Toronto and Vancouver, a salary of $70,000 to $80,000 is often considered
    necessary for a comfortable single-person lifestyle, while in smaller cities and rural areas, $50,000
    can provide a similar standard of living.
  </p>
  <p>
    Household income is often a more meaningful measure. The median household income in Canada is
    approximately $72,000, meaning that combined earnings from all household members place half of Canadian
    households above this threshold.
  </p>

  <h2>Salary Growth Trends</h2>
  <p>
    Average wages in Canada have grown by approximately 3 to 5 percent annually in recent years, driven by
    tight labour markets, minimum wage increases, and inflation. However, after adjusting for inflation
    (real wage growth), the gains have been more modest. The post-pandemic period (2022-2025) saw above-average
    nominal wage growth as employers competed for talent, particularly in technology, healthcare, and skilled
    trades. Looking ahead, wage growth is expected to moderate as inflation cools and the labour market
    reaches a more balanced state.
  </p>

